{
  "dom_id": "step-q129440",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$\\sqrt{49{h}^{2}{k}^{2}}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $49,{h}^{2},$ and ${k}^{2}$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49{h}^{2}{k}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}\\cdot {h}^{2}\\cdot {k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7\\cdot 7\\cdot h\\cdot h\\cdot k\\cdot k} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7\\cdot h\\cdot k\\cdot 7\\cdot h\\cdot k} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7hk)\\cdot (7hk)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7hk)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(7hk)^{2}} & =|7hk| \\\\  & =|7|\\cdot |hk| \\\\  & =7|hk|\\end{aligned}\n$$",
